  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Informal workers in Zimbabwe advocate for systems change to value women work.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This is a continuation of SACA69 project. Initially, working to reduce reducing the drudgery of certain care tasks and the time and labour required to complete these tasks like laundry, fuel collection and cooking requires investment into care services and infrastructure, as well as working towards influencing policymakers, governments, private sector and IFIs to see care work as a policy and economic developmental issue. However this is not adequate, as  there is still need to redefine what &apos;work&apos; means so all the work that women do  - paid and unpaid can deserve to be recognised, fairly measured and rewarded,  invested in as part of the global economy. Working with informal workers organisations, social movements and Women&apos;s Right Organisations (WROs) to  build and strengthen their capacity to advocate and influence, governments and policy makers to measure and count women&apos;s work as a valuable contributing to national economies. This will provide a basis for engaging key actors such as governments, private sector, United Nation (UN) agencies and communities to dialogue, debate, research and develop policy interventions so that societies can truly value women&apos;s work, and recognise the contribution of women measure and value, this as part of integrating the regional economy.</narrative>
  </description>
